{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run2:inflation:reconciler","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:068063f29d431165e2aca5c9ff3233d938eeecba0a0ef89bdcd906469ce53f50","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"quantiles","quantiles":[{"probability":0.1,"value":0.85},{"probability":0.5,"value":2.2},{"probability":0.9,"value":3.8}]},"questionHash":"sha256:20d8e05d3bea415eb4452ff8b599c74a3c3ce1a1b0c2ce11dda57a4e19490197","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq4","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"Wave P50s 2.10 / 2.10 / 2.15 / 2.15 / 2.35 — four lenses inside 5bp of each other and of their own computed anchors, which the Red-Teamer correctly called anchoring dressed as humility.\nANCHOR = 1.95%. This is the highest-quality single piece of work in the effort and it is the Bayesian Updater's: CQ4 asks for the G7 GDP-WEIGHTED 10y real yield, not the US TIPS 2.60% that 2A locks, and Fermi's 1.72% assumed Japanese and German real legs of 0.3 and 0.5 that the Scout's own observed 2026 telemetry has already falsified (Bund 10y \"reached 3.28% on August 18, its highest since May 5, 2011\"; JGB 10y \"had earlier hit 3.000% on Tuesday - the first time since 1996\"; US 30y \"closed at 5.31% on August 17, its highest since June 12, 2007\"). Net of ~1.9-2.0% breakevens those imply non-US G7 reals of ~1.0-1.3%, +80bp on Fermi's assumption. Re-anchored: US 2.60x0.46 + JP 1.10x0.12 + DE 1.30x0.11 + UK 1.50x0.08 + FR 1.20x0.08 + IT 2.00x0.07 + CA 1.60x0.055 = 1.96% normalized. Market prices beat reconstructed anchors; I adopt 1.95 and note the Scout's caveat that no current G7-aggregate real-yield series was found, so every anchor here is an assembled weighting of national partials of differing dates.\nDEMOGRAPHIC DRIFT -0.10, AND HERE I DO NOT MOVE WITH THE RED TEAM. He argued that Fermi's identity dr = d(r-g) + dg = +1.0 - 1.1 = -0.1 is \"a function of the number I am attacking\" and substituted his own total growth decline (-0.6pp) to get dr = +0.4pp. That is a category error, not a hit: the locked statement is about the DEMOGRAPHIC partial effects — aging worsens (r-g) by ~+1.0pp while slowing growth by ~1.1pp, \"even where a frictionless r* eases\" — so the demographic contribution to r is ~-0.1pp by the prior's own construction. Substituting a total realized growth change for a demographic partial mixes two different counterfactuals. This is the clearest instance in Wave 2 of contrarian posing rather than a landed hit, and I leave the term at -0.10.\nOTHER r* TERMS, adopted from Fermi and unchallenged: public dissaving/duration supply +0.40 (debt >100% of GDP by 2029, ~142% US / ~127% China by 2031, net interest 2% -> 3% of GDP, eroding convenience yields, levered basis-trade absorption, G7-weighted down for domestic and central absorption in Japan and Germany); AI/grid capex demand for savings +0.25 (gross +55 to +110bp on ~+1.1pp of GDP of incremental investment demand, haircut because the locked ~$1.4T of commitments on ~$300B equity and ~$650B debt leaves only ~$450B unfunded against a ~$30T/yr global saving pool, and AI-Q4's 66% withdraws part mid-window); precautionary saving -0.30; cognitive labour-share compression into high-propensity-to-save capital owners -0.15. Net r* = +0.10.\nTERM PREMIUM +0.45. I reject the Bayesian's halving to +0.35: two reputable houses on opposite signs about a ONE-MONTH move in the JGB term premium (Macquarie, 1 Oct 2026, \"the 10-year JGB term premium has risen by around 84 bps since the Takaichi government was elected\"; MUFG, 26 Oct 2026, \"narrowing of term premium excluding asset swap spread was contributing to a decline in the 10-year JGB yield\") is a 1:1 signal about a month, not about a decade, and spending it against a one-directional structural case is a misapplication of good discipline. The structural case is in the locked priors and is not in dispute: 3-year P95 Debt-at-Risk ~117% of GDP and +2.4pp under a 20% AI-led equity shock, convenience-yield erosion, dealer balance sheets that have not scaled with a roughly doubled float, QE/QT regime switching, BoJ normalization.\nREPRESSION WEDGE -0.15, cut from Fermi's -0.25. The Red-Teamer's hit is real: Fermi applied -100bp to Japan at an 11-13% weight, and the Scout's observed 3.00% JGB is the data that falsifies that assumption. Japan normalizing from ~1.0% to ~1.9% real is +10 to +12bp on the aggregate AND removes Fermi's -11bp credit, a ~22bp swing from one leg.\nBREAKEVEN UNDER-ADJUSTMENT -0.20. The measure is nominal minus breakeven/TIPS. If my hot-core call is right (conjunct A at 0.64) and breakevens persistently under-adjust to realized inflation, the MEASURED real yield falls even in a hot nominal regime. Only the Scout named this and only the Red-Teamer priced it against his own thesis; it is the one channel in the effort that mechanically damps a hawkish view and it is live precisely because my CQ2 is high.\nP50 = 1.95 + 0.10 + 0.45 - 0.15 - 0.20 = 2.15 -> 2.20, nudged up because the anchor is now pinned by market prices rather than reconstructed and the term-premium add is structural rather than telemetric.\nP90 = 3.80. I adopt the Red-Teamer's methodological catch: Historian justified a 3.60 tail by noting that 4 of 5 G7 decade-to-decade transitions since 1970 moved >=1.5pp, then set a tail only 1.6pp above his start. From 1.95, the 1980s/1990s term-premium regime without successful repression — convenience yield gone, basis trade unwinding into forced selling, a US real of 4.0-4.5% dragging the periphery — is +1.85pp, well inside the transition distribution he himself cited. P10 = 0.85: capex bust plus demand shortfall, savings glut compressing r*, flight to duration, Japanese and German reals back toward zero, plus successful repression, which is a POLICY-made left tail rather than a market one. Strongly right-skewed: the debt arithmetic is one-directional and the zero lower bound is no longer the binding floor it was in the 2010s.\nCOHERENCE WITH CQ3 (r - g arithmetic), stated as the lock demands: CQ4 P50 2.20% real against CQ3 P50 1.75% real growth gives r - g = +0.45pp, a configuration in which debt/GDP does NOT stabilize through the denominator — which is why my CQ6-A is only 15 and why CQ6-B's \"steep real term premia\" language is the modal description rather than A's \"debt/GDP stabilizes via growth\". It is also directionally consistent with the locked \"aging worsens global (r-g) by ~+1.0pp\".","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:57.328Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"Turkiye annual CPI (TurkStat, December print) averages below 8.0%/yr over calendar 2028-2031 AND Mexico's headline CPI (INEGI) averages below 3.5% over the same four years. Both required, so a single good Turkish year does not trigger it.\",\"by_when\":\"Feb 28, 2032 (full-year 2031 releases)\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies the single load-bearing term in my CQ2: the five high-inflation OECD-38 members contributing ~0.70pp to GDP-weighted core. That contribution would fall toward ~0.40pp and my 2027-2036 aggregate from 2.65% to ~2.35%. CQ2 conjunct A falls from 0.64 to ~0.42 and CQ2 from 32 to ~21; CQ6-C falls from 22 to ~16 and CQ6-D rises from 13 to ~18, with B absorbing the balance; CQ4 P50 falls ~0.15pp to ~2.05; hypothesis plank (c) rises from 45 to ~58. My whole divergence from the Historian's CQ2 of 14 would stand revealed as a weighting convention rather than a finding.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"BLS PPI for software publishers AND for computer systems design, plus the euro-area HICP professional-services sub-indices, show OUTRIGHT annual declines of 1.0% or more for two consecutive years in those market-priced (not cost- or asset-fee-imputed) series.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies the half of the Red-Teamer's imputation attack that I accepted — that the genuinely deflatable slice of core is only 0.08-0.12 rather than 0.20-0.25 and is partly counter-cyclically hot. My 2032-36 core central falls from 2.45% to ~2.25%, taking P(core >= 2.70%) from 0.27 to ~0.14: CQ6-C falls from 22 to ~14, B rises to ~56, D rises to ~15, A unchanged at 15; CQ2 falls to ~25. The Wave-1 unanimous 'extreme skew, dull aggregate' thesis would be vindicated against both me and the Red Team, and he would be the effort's worst input rather than its best corrective.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"China headline CPI (NBS), three-year average over calendar 2031-2033, exceeds 2.2%/yr, with the services sub-index above the goods sub-index in at least two of those three years. My split-window build is 2027-2030 at ~1.0% and 2031-2036 at ~1.9-2.1%, giving a decade average knife-edge against the 1.50% bar.\",\"by_when\":\"Mar 31, 2034\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Collapses CQ2's China conjunct from 0.47 to ~0.18, taking CQ2 from 32 to ~17. It also means the Chinese labour-scarcity services-wage channel turned reflationary earlier than the locked 2A arithmetic implies, which raises China's growth leg: CQ3 P50 rises ~0.10pp to ~1.85 and P10 to ~1.0, CQ6-D falls ~3 to 10 and A rises ~2 to 17. The services-above-goods condition is there so that a hog cycle or an oil shock alone does not fire it.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Count of OECD-38 members that enact a binding national or sector-wide care-wage award worth 5% or more in real terms, cumulatively over 2027-2031 (tracked in OECD Health at a Glance and national LTC reimbursement schedules), reaches six or more — against zero or one as the null.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"This is the cheapest available test of the mechanism on which I overrode the Bayesian Updater's CQ1 of 8, namely that administered care prices move in discrete statutory level jumps rather than never. Six or more awards takes CQ1's second conjunct from 0.22 to ~0.33 and CQ1 from 16 to ~24, raises plank (a) from 6 to ~12, and adds ~0.08pp to my core path via the ISIC Q channel (CQ2 to ~34, CQ6-C to ~24). Zero or one takes conjunct B to ~0.12 and CQ1 to ~10, i.e. all the way back to the Wave-1 centre, and vindicates the pure fiscal-quantity-rationing read.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"ICE BofA MOVE calendar-year average stays below 85 in every year 2027-2031 while the G7 GDP-weighted 10y nominal sovereign yield averages above 4.0% across the same five years. Both required: a low-vol, high-rate regime is the configuration that kills the level-scaling term.\",\"by_when\":\"Jan 31, 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies the baseline assumption behind my CQ5 conjunct A of 0.37 — that a ~85-95 structural baseline plus one 12-18 month crisis clears a decade mean of 105 — and vindicates the Scout's reading that the 2026 compression at 64-71 with nominals already at 3-4% is structural rather than policy-certainty-driven. CQ5 falls from 27 to ~15; CQ4 P90 falls from 3.80 to ~3.30 and P50 to ~2.05 as the term-premium add is shown to be unwarranted; CQ6-C falls ~4 to 18 with B absorbing it. The reverse — MOVE averaging above 110 in both 2029 and 2030 — takes CQ5 to ~38 and CQ4 P50 to ~2.50.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run2 wave 3 reconciler seat, effort 'inflation'."}}